    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>North-facing rooms are often the most misunderstood spaces in a home. While south-facing rooms bask in golden, direct sun, north-facing rooms receive a consistent but cool, bluish light that stays steady throughout the day. This lack of direct sunlight can make a room feel chilly, flat or even gloomy if it is not handled well. With the right strategy for paint and artificial lighting, though, these rooms can become cosy, sophisticated and wonderfully atmospheric.</p>
<p>A quick note on geography: this guide is written for the northern hemisphere, including the UK. South of the equator it reverses, so it is a south-facing room that gets this cool, indirect light.</p>

<h2 id="natural-light">1. Natural light</h2>
<p>Understanding the physics of northern light is the first step towards decorating success. Unlike the shifting shadows and strong sun of southern or western exposures, northern light is indirect. It does not create harsh glare or "hot spots" on the furniture. Instead, it provides very reliable, even illumination.</p>
<p>Artists have long favoured north light for studios because it does not change colour much as the sun moves across the sky. For a living space, however, this reliable light is inherently cool, with a blue-grey undertone. Put a standard cool white paint in a north-facing room and it can easily look like a hospital corridor or a cold garage. The goal is not necessarily to fight the light, but to work with its temperature to create a sense of balance.</p>

<h2 id="warm-undertones">2. Warm undertones</h2>
<p>The golden rule for north-facing rooms is to prioritise warmth. Because the natural light leans towards the blue end of the spectrum, introduce red, yellow or orange undertones to neutralise the chill.</p>
<p>Warm whites are the most popular choice for these spaces. Look for whites with a hint of yellow or pink. They catch the diffuse light and bounce it around, so the room feels glowing rather than sterile. Avoid paints with a blue or green base, as the northern light amplifies them and the room can end up feeling muddy or drab. Even a "neutral" grey can look surprisingly lilac or slate-blue in a north-facing bedroom.</p>
<p>So always test before you commit. A tiny tester square tells you very little: paint large swatches, at least A3 size (30 × 42 cm, about 12 × 16.5 in), with two coats, on boards or lining paper you can move around. Check them on the window wall and in the darkest corner, in the morning, the afternoon and the evening under your own lamps, and on a grey day as well as a bright one.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Compare whites side by side against a sheet of plain printer paper. Undertones are hard to see on their own, but next to pure white the yellow, pink or blue base becomes obvious.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="bold-pigmentation">3. Bold pigmentation</h2>
<p>One of the biggest mistakes people make with dark rooms is painting them brilliant white to "brighten" them. Without direct sunlight, white paint often looks grey and flat. Sometimes the best approach is to lean into the lack of light and go bold.</p>
<p>Deep, saturated colours such as forest green, navy blue or charcoal work very well in north-facing rooms. They do not rely on the sun to look good; instead, they create a "jewel-box" effect. A colour with plenty of pigment embraces the shadows and makes a room that feels intentional and cosy. Combined with warm wood furniture and brass hardware, a dark north-facing room becomes a sophisticated retreat that feels like a warm hug.</p>

<h2 id="reflective-finishes">4. Reflective finishes</h2>
<p>The finish of your paint, also known as the sheen, plays a big part in how light travels through a north-facing room. A flat or matt finish absorbs light, which can make a dim room feel even more closed in.</p>
<p>In spaces with limited natural light, consider moving up the sheen scale. An eggshell or satin finish on the walls gives a subtle lustre that helps bounce the available light back into the room. A semi-gloss or high-gloss finish reflects even more, so it can make a ceiling or woodwork feel noticeably brighter. Bear in mind that gloss shows every bump, crack and patch on walls and ceilings, so it suits smooth, well-prepared surfaces and woodwork such as skirting, doors and frames best.</p>

<h2 id="layered-lighting">5. Layered lighting</h2>
<p>Because natural light is weak in a north-facing room, your artificial lighting has to do the heavy lifting. Relying on a single overhead "big light" is a recipe for a flat, uninviting space. Instead, layer your lighting.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ambient lighting</strong> provides general illumination, such as a ceiling pendant, chandelier or downlights.</li>
<li><strong>Task lighting</strong> serves specific activities, such as reading lamps or under-cabinet lights.</li>
<li><strong>Accent lighting</strong> highlights architectural features or artwork, such as picture lights or LED strips.</li>
</ul>
<p>With several light sources at different heights, you create highlights and shadows that give the room depth and character, echoing the play of light in a sunnier space.</p>

<h2 id="warm-bulbs">6. Warm bulbs</h2>
<p>The colour temperature of your bulbs matters just as much as the colour of your paint. It is measured in kelvin (K). In north-facing rooms, avoid bulbs in the "daylight" range (5000–6500K) for living spaces and bedrooms. They imitate bright, bluish daylight, which adds to the already cool natural light and makes the room feel clinical.</p>
<p>Opt for warm white bulbs, typically 2700–3000K. They give a yellowish glow closer to sunset or firelight, filling the gap left by the missing southern sun, flattering warm-toned paint colours and creating a sense of comfort. In a north-facing home office you might go up to around 3500K for clarity, but keep the decorative lamps nearer 2700K. Check your paint swatches under these bulbs in the evening too, as colours can shift under artificial light.</p>

<h2 id="strategic-mirrors">7. Strategic mirrors</h2>
<!--figure:living-room-round-mirror-cream-sofa|A cream living room styled with a round mirror, warm artwork, plants and a pedestal table.-->
<p>Mirrors are one of the oldest tricks in the designer's handbook for a reason: they work. In a north-facing room, where you put them is a strategic decision.</p>
<p>To make the most of the light, place a large mirror opposite or beside the window. It catches the incoming cool light and reflects it onto the warmer-toned walls. If a large mirror does not suit your style, consider mirrored furniture, metallic accents or glass-fronted cabinets. Each reflective surface acts as a relay station for light, helping it travel further into the dark corners of the room. Fix heavy mirrors securely with fixings suited to the wall and the mirror's weight.</p>

<h2 id="window-treatments">8. Window treatments</h2>
<p>When light is limited, the last thing you want to do is block it. Thick, heavy or dark curtains can swallow the little light a north-facing room receives.</p>
<p>Opt for light-filtering window treatments. Sheer linen or voile curtains are ideal because they give privacy while letting plenty of light through. If you need more light control at night, layer them: a sheer for the day and a heavier curtain on a pole that extends beyond the frame, so it can be pulled completely clear of the glass in daylight hours. That way all of the window is working to bring brightness into your home.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A north-facing room will never behave like a sunny south-facing one, and it does not need to. Choose warm undertones or commit to deep, enveloping colour, test large swatches at different times of day, and let warm, layered lighting, mirrors and light-filtering curtains do the rest. Worked with rather than against, that cool, steady light becomes one of the calmest and most atmospheric in the house.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="grey-paint-north-facing">Can I ever use grey paint in a north-facing room?</h3>
<p>Yes, but be careful. Avoid cool greys with blue or green bases. Instead, look for greige (a mix of grey and beige) or greys with yellow or red undertones. These warm greys are far less likely to turn blue or purple in the cool light. Test them with large swatches first.</p>
<h3 id="north-facing-bedroom">Is a north-facing room good for a bedroom?</h3>
<p>It can be a lovely choice. Many people like north-facing bedrooms because the light is consistent and does not stream in with bright sun and glare first thing in the morning. It gives a calm, steady feel that suits a room for resting and relaxing.</p>
<h3 id="best-white-north-facing-kitchen">What is the best white paint for a north-facing kitchen?</h3>
<p>Look for what designers often call creamy whites or off-whites: whites with a yellow or red undertone. They have enough warmth to stop the kitchen feeling like a cold laboratory. Compare a few side by side and test them on the wall under both daylight and your kitchen lighting.</p>
<h3 id="lumens-north-facing-living-room">How many lumens do I need for a north-facing living room?</h3>
<p>As a rough starting point, aim for around 100–200 lumens per square metre (roughly 10–20 per sq ft) for a living room, spread across several sources rather than one ceiling light. North-facing rooms can feel darker, so lean towards the upper end and add lamps and accent lights so the space feels bright enough in the evenings. Dimmers let you adjust the level to the time of day.</p>
<h3 id="plants-north-facing-rooms">Do plants grow well in north-facing rooms?</h3>
<p>Some do. North-facing windows give low to medium indirect light, so choose plants that cope with those conditions. Snake plants, ZZ plants, peace lilies and pothos are popular low-light choices, but they are toxic to cats and dogs, so keep them out of homes with pets or well out of reach. Pet-safer low-light options commonly listed as non-toxic include the parlour palm and cast-iron plant; check the ASPCA's toxic and non-toxic plant list before buying, and see our guide to <a href="/blog/non-toxic-decor-for-cats">non-toxic decor for cats</a>. Avoid succulents and flowering plants that need full sun.</p>

